#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133711 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f4f726e-d985-5915-a4d0-30cfa6f4b89b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"}] :question/id #uuid "a817c3c6-97bb-55e4-9a1e-04ed99d3eaab" :question/math-academy-id "e-9553" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ which of the following could be the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\left\\langle -y, \\: y\\right\\rangle$\n\nII. $\\left\\langle -2x, \\: 2x\\right\\rangle$\n\nIII. $\\left\\langle 5y\\sin x, \\: x-5\\cos x \\right\\rangle$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle P(x,y), \\: Q(x,y) \\right\\rangle,$ where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case, \n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -1$\nand\n$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does not give the required area. In this case, \n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0$\nand\n$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 5\\sin x$\nand\n$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 1+5\\sin x,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8644454b-ee25-59fd-ae65-9770a0b44442" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "74c9ecfb-5005-53ff-b7a9-db798488587b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9554" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2 \\cos t \\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n![](images/45/45513e4aedd37b868d2020cbbb47f49938f6ca03487ded8b5e519a2ec8e4edf5.svg)\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*\n\n![](images/a5/a5eeb5db90f9ee0d86a49e5bbf02629c7886fa019db7cff3c4d0e1eb43e8fd75.png)" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n- \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = \\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'(t) = \\left\\langle  2\\cos t ,\\:  -2 \\sin t \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $4\\cos^2 t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133713 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "aa4400ff-09b0-596c-9857-c13881b72f73" :question/math-academy-id "e-9555" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C=C_1 \\cup C_2,$ where \n\n- $C_1$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}_1(t) =-2 t^4 \\: \\mathbf{i} + t^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in \\left[-1,1\\right],$ and\n\n- $C_2$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}_2(t) = -2\\: \\mathbf{i} -t \\: \\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in \\left[-1,1\\right].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region.\n\n![](images/14/147e8fdd97fc938ae5482b70d7215483956a860ee3ce69ae5bbebd111dcc343b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of $\\mathbf r_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf r_2(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-8{t}^{3}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot (-8{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t+0 \\\\  & =8[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f4f726e-d985-5915-a4d0-30cfa6f4b89b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f503e12-e11c-54c7-8ba7-5830ef6f73bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431014} {:answer/id #uuid "20a55ace-f5e1-5a5a-91ce-2c0b7d1404a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431015} {:answer/id #uuid "4798dafc-c73d-5ec4-b12f-da6f6620e4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431022} {:answer/id #uuid "8a583e9f-a399-590a-ac83-6aef2ccb73c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431030} {:answer/id #uuid "fac08602-5de8-5d47-96f6-859f484e607d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4798dafc-c73d-5ec4-b12f-da6f6620e4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431022} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3715f35-6d8a-57c2-a089-fd1bb9bddf47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d67db6a-7337-599e-8ecc-26284642ae3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126574" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨y{e}^{x},\\,x-{e}^{x}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨2y,\\,x⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{2x}{3}+1⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1-{e}^{x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-2{e}^{x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{3}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2}{3},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f4f726e-d985-5915-a4d0-30cfa6f4b89b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de6da70-c426-566d-b3d0-dce7471f5600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431013} {:answer/id #uuid "576c0bfb-effc-5ce5-827d-af6530c65e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431025} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d96a71-7dc4-568f-af4e-fef495f9c448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431033} {:answer/id #uuid "cd786957-3ffe-55c0-a40e-e21f738990d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431035} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb13b39-dc15-5bed-8347-e772308dd574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "576c0bfb-effc-5ce5-827d-af6530c65e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431025} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd054105-4a4d-5e12-a937-81dcca4212b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c0dea55-217c-592d-9a6c-d305c8e67729" :question/math-academy-id "q-126575" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3},\\,{x}^{3}+1⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{2}+\\sqrt{2}x,\\,\\frac{3x}{2}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨x,\\,x⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{3}{2},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ee80ff-c5ae-55ec-a96c-b506acfb7915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431021} {:answer/id #uuid "4addbefd-201e-5755-8641-7e27605c3311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431023} {:answer/id #uuid "7a937f5d-4690-55ac-bcf9-5662d89ca2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431027} {:answer/id #uuid "b4200cd7-229f-5b2c-80d8-6fbc6195eb39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431034} {:answer/id #uuid "dd351131-c322-54cb-8179-38615de2670f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a937f5d-4690-55ac-bcf9-5662d89ca2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431027} :answer-field/id #uuid "771735d3-fa1a-5273-a935-b05b06edb2c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "819104c2-11eb-5b49-9398-d7d5879660f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126727" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b51e252935f7077f3285258845d8d1e4957b83894037f968ee306910aa88bad6.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8644454b-ee25-59fd-ae65-9770a0b44442" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30625b21-0638-5df6-8e7f-5ecca67ca674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431017} {:answer/id #uuid "31a713ed-2a9a-58c6-bfc8-e17df3a2f279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431018} {:answer/id #uuid "7643c9ff-155d-5092-a735-8716263f25b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431026} {:answer/id #uuid "a4547936-89a5-5eb1-a5c2-aca7ffed8e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431032} {:answer/id #uuid "f95f8500-94e5-5ec5-b5e0-95f0daf53e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f95f8500-94e5-5ec5-b5e0-95f0daf53e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431039} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9269d5-fd3f-54ff-be8f-682c742d6ce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2177a684-6e86-5255-ac01-04a426569ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126760" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a95b161dab89fe1245724921ef4ee36112d7a4062e7e171411e4ffe61253b91f.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8644454b-ee25-59fd-ae65-9770a0b44442" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031791c1-0f2f-546c-82e8-999c8554dc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{cos}^{6}⁡t{sin}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431012} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf73cdb-b201-56ec-9e65-ae9499b1e5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{cos}^{5}⁡t{sin}^{5}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431028} {:answer/id #uuid "88f605ff-e892-57f0-8a1b-c76ad9f5a91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{cos}^{5}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431029} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ec8ccb-1292-5505-905e-84a2ec107ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{sin}^{6}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431037} {:answer/id #uuid "f58f79ef-051d-5166-b8fb-70ef66a978d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{sin}^{6}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f58f79ef-051d-5166-b8fb-70ef66a978d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{sin}^{6}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431038} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e0a66d-4420-5ec9-9e88-13a5cdd58112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1f25e51-cfc7-5f63-98bf-d803b12546e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126761" :question/problem "![](images/10/10f4094edb09afc7908bd0cd3df089387c8f5b762ea5ef86da152d1c9d08b5e4.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{sin}^{5}⁡t,\\,{cos}^{5}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨5{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{sin}^{5}⁡t)\\cdot (-5{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5{sin}^{6}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b151d74-5c74-545d-8b07-dea1d8453657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431016} {:answer/id #uuid "32eb8a32-3dbf-536c-8383-dfb162184c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431019} {:answer/id #uuid "419ff17a-c941-551d-ad1c-89eccf44a609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431020} {:answer/id #uuid "4b345484-1be0-5dbb-8ec3-b21aa5962d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431024} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a3e7c7-ac4d-53f3-be35-8650eb6afd4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b345484-1be0-5dbb-8ec3-b21aa5962d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431024} :answer-field/id #uuid "64517546-ac9e-59a3-822e-98cda097a61b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd34f4a5-5a97-5165-88b5-594ad30569e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-126859" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ebabcf1e3c3c3d2f4850d425657e7314c0135c81a432bfbbc8ae31ee39cb85d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)={t}^{5}\\,i-2{t}^{4}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=-t\\,i-2\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}\\,i-8{t}^{3}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}x\\,dy-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\cdot (-8{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{8}\\,\\text{d}t+0 \\\\  & =8[\\frac{{t}^{9}}{9}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
